S('ch09','SI_erosion_units',r'where \$v_e\$ is the erosional velocity \(m/s\).*?60–80% of \$v_e\$\.',r'For the conventional field-unit coefficient $C$, this displayed equation gives ft/s when density is lb/ft³. In SI use $v_e=1.2193C/\sqrt{\rho_m}$ in m/s with density in kg/m³. The C-factor and any operating fraction are service assumptions; this screen does not establish sand erosion, corrosion or vibration acceptance.')
S('ch09','FIV_AIV',r'\*\*Flow-induced vibration \(FIV\)\*\* constraints.*?small-bore piping causes acoustic-induced vibration\.', '**Flow-induced vibration (FIV)** includes low-frequency structural response to turbulent or multiphase excitation. **Acoustic-induced vibration (AIV)** is a distinct high-frequency shell response often associated with high-energy gas pressure reduction. Velocity alone does not distinguish or accept either mechanism.')
R('ch09','corrosion_exercise','identify the temperature at which maximum corrosion occurs for each CO$_2$ level','show that this uncorrected relation increases monotonically with temperature at fixed CO$_2$ pressure. Explain why protective-film, pH and transport effects are needed to predict a nonmonotonic field trend')
S('ch11','reboiler_energy',r'\*\*Reboiler duty\*\*:.*?(?=### 11\.4\.6)',r'''**Reboiler duty** determines vapor traffic, but bottoms flow is not itself a vaporization rate. For the complete steady column, define positive $Q_R$ into the reboiler and positive $Q_C$ removed in the condenser:

$$Q_R-Q_C=\dot H_D+\dot H_B-\dot H_F.$$

All enthalpy flows are in W on one reference basis. An unrefluxed column has $Q_C=0$; any feed heater is outside this boundary. For the reboiler alone, use its incoming liquid, outgoing vapor and bottoms enthalpies. The checked examples specify heat input directly and verify the individual-stage and complete-column balance.

''')
R('ch11','D341_blending','for more accurate results, the Walther equation or ASTM D341 chart method should be used.','ASTM D341/Walther fits viscosity versus temperature for a given petroleum liquid, not arbitrary blend viscosity. Use measured blend data or a validated blending correlation at the same temperature; logarithmic mixing is only a screening estimate.')
R('ch11','Coriolis_proving','Direct mass measurement, no prover needed','Direct mass measurement; calibration/proving requirements still apply')
R('ch11','K_meter_factor','meter factor (K-factor) of a flow meter','meter calibration. A K-factor is pulses per unit volume; a meter factor is a dimensionless correction to indicated quantity')
S('ch11','meter_equation',r'The meter factor is calculated as:.*?(?=### 11\.10\.4)',r'''With both volumes corrected to one common basis, distinguish

$$K=\frac{N_{pulses}}{V_{reference}},\qquad MF=\frac{V_{reference}}{V_{indicated}}.$$

The first has units pulses/m³ and the second is dimensionless. A prover calculation includes calibrated base volume, steel temperature/pressure corrections and fluid temperature/pressure corrections as applicable; multiplying only the base volume by fluid CTL/CPL is not a complete proving procedure.

''')
